$DIR/main.p:26:8: x86.InterruptGate cannot be deferred: it must be called with the name of a handler, and its result used
    x86.InterruptGate and x86.TrapGate build the entry of the interrupt table
    that enters a handler, and the compiler puts the handler's address in it. So
    the first argument must be the name of a handler, known when the program is
    compiled: a function marked //pu:interrupt for InterruptGate, and a function
    marked //pu:interrupt trap for TrapGate. A func value, a deferred or started
    call, a handler of the other form, a call through a variable and any other
    expression are errors.
    The form decides how the processor enters the handler. An interrupt gate
    clears the interrupt flag, and a handler of the plain form relies on that:
    its body is a region with interrupts disabled. A trap gate leaves the flag
    as it was, which is what a handler that can be entered again needs.
    Pass the name of a handler of the form the constructor takes:
        //pu:interrupt
        func timer(f *x86.InterruptFrame) { ... }

        idt[32] = x86.InterruptGate(timer, 0x08, 0)
    (see "pluto/arch/x86" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.8)
    (run "pluto explain E2572" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:27:8: x86.InterruptGate cannot be deferred: it must be called with the name of a handler, and its result used
    (explained above; run "pluto explain E2572" for more)

$DIR/main.p:28:8: x86.TrapGate cannot be deferred: it must be called with the name of a handler, and its result used
    (explained above; run "pluto explain E2572" for more)

$DIR/main.p:30:2: interrupt handler main.spaced cannot be called; use x86.InterruptGate or x86.TrapGate to enter it in the interrupt table
    An interrupt handler saves every register and returns with iretq, so
    ordinary code cannot call it, and it is not an ordinary func value. Use
    x86.InterruptGate (or x86.TrapGate for a //pu:interrupt trap handler) to
    enter it in the interrupt table.
    Enter it in the table:
        idt[32] = x86.InterruptGate(timer, 0x08, 0)
    (see "Interrupts" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.10)
    (run "pluto explain E2573" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:31:6: x86.InterruptGate: spaced is a //pu:interrupt trap function; install it with x86.TrapGate
    (explained above; run "pluto explain E2572" for more)
